The upstream mirror is archived and has no tags, so the module is vendored
rather than fetched. The copy is a package of arcctl's module, not a module
of its own: `go install` refuses a go.mod with replace directives, and this
way arcctl's vet, staticcheck and tests cover the patched code. Everything
here is upstream code except the local patches in patches/, applied in
order, and two mechanical steps: the import path in example_test.go is
rewritten to this package's, and go.mod and go.sum are removed.

  0001-setreport-timeout.patch  darwin: pass a 1 s timeout to
      IOHIDDeviceSetReportWithCallback so IOKit completes a report the device
      never accepts with kIOReturnTimeout. The argument is a CFTimeInterval,
      but IOKit reads it as milliseconds, so the value passed is 1000. Also
      stop waiting for the completion once the device is closing, so Close
      cannot hang behind a stuck write. A report still in flight after a
      grace period is abandoned and its buffer leaked rather than freed while
      IOKit may use it.
  0002-buffered-input.patch     darwin: queue up to 64 input reports in front
      of GetInputReport instead of dropping every report that arrives while
      the reader is busy, and count the reports dropped when the queue is
      full (Device.DroppedInputReports).
  0003-report-descriptor.patch  darwin and Linux: keep the report descriptor
      read at enumeration and return it from Device.ReportDescriptor, so
      arcctl can check that an interface declares the vendor channel before
      it sends anything to it.
  0004-lint.patch               make the package pass arcctl's vet and
      staticcheck: a test formatted uint16 values with %q, and the padding
      fields of a C struct mirror become blank fields (same layout).

Patches 1 to 3 add tests that run against stubbed IOKit calls or plain values.
To check the copy, or to re-vendor after changing a patch, run
patches/verify.sh (see its header). arcctl's docs/decisions.md records why
the patches exist.
